<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="3852d0d1">&lt;p&gt;
    Plan to hold a meeting to discuss refinement at least once per sprint; however, the process could also be segmented
    into multiple smaller meetings that recur throughout the course of the sprint. Typically, one or more of the
    below&amp;nbsp;structures are employed:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        1-2 meetings each week focused on refinement, on agreed upon days
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Workshop during the last week of the sprint to refine the Product Backlog
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Ongoing refinement where the team works closely with the Product Owner and stakeholders to gain knowledge on an
        ongoing basis
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Consult and agree together as a team on the most appropriate format.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Invite stakeholders to participate in the refinement meeting in addition to the Scrum team. Schedule a recurring
    meeting for everyone&apos;s convenience and have the Scrum Master facilitate the conversation between the Product Owner and
    the team. Use the refinement meeting time to:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Add details to existing Backlog Items (e.g., User Stories, features); refine descriptions, personas or acceptance
        criteria
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Add any newly identified Backlog Items to the Product Backlog
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Remove any Backlog Items that are no longer applicable
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Split Backlog Items as needed, especially those that have been deemed too large to complete as-is
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Merge Backlog Items, for example when the goals of two or more items can be achieved by the same work performed by
        the Development Team
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Re-order the Backlog Items as more information is learned about stakeholders&apos; needs and requirements
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Next, review existing or newly identified dependencies. Once all dependencies have been resolved, let the Development
    Team estimate the work effort of the items that have just been refined. The Product Backlog, at least at the end of the
    sprint, should contain a set of small Backlog Items ordered at the top which are understood by the team well enough to
    satisfy the conditions in the Definition of Ready.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Note, the Product Owner is the sole person responsible for the Product Backlog and therefore no changes can be made to
    it without the Product Owner&apos;s consent. The only exception to this is re-estimation of work as done by the Development
    Team.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    As important as it is to have the top of the Product Backlog ready for the next sprint planning meeting, the
    participants should also refine the remainder of the Product Backlog. This ongoing exercise is important so that as
    Backlog Items are completed and removed from the Product Backlog there is already some understanding of the next set of
    Backlog Items to be worked on. Refine the Product Backlog in this manner&amp;nbsp;so it is well balanced with a mix of
    Backlog Items understood to varying extents.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="ecaf999c"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="2f194dc">Work together as a team to determine how and when to refine the Product Backlog. The timing of this meeting will vary
depending on the context of the engagement and how the team chooses to approach this exercise.</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="36647a6c"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="fe3eb68f">To learn more about the stakeholders&apos; goals and needs and make sure they are accurately reflected in the Product Backlog.</attribute>
<attribute name="alternatives" nodeId="92fd413"></attribute>
</Element>
